th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/ratio-test.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/ratio-test.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_ratio_test_64a97320_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In the ratio test, what limit is considered for $\\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty} a_n$?","options":["$\\displaystyle L = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}|a_n|$","$\\displaystyle L = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}|a_{n+1}-a_n|$","$\\displaystyle L = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\ \\left|\\frac{a_n}{a_{n+1}}\\right|$","$\\displaystyle L = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\ \\left|\\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\\right|$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5a0500534437ac2247fe12bd","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_ratio_test_64a97320_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For a series $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ with eventually nonzero terms, suppose $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}|a_{n+1}/a_n|=1/\\mathrm{e}$. What does the ratio test imply about convergence of this series?","options":["The series converges, but not absolutely","The test is inconclusive","The series converges absolutely","The series diverges, since $L\\neq0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3eb1f4b0884cc5b09e76d1cb","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_ratio_test_64a97320_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $L = 2$ in the ratio test, what is the conclusion?","options":["The series diverges","The series converges absolutely","The test proves uniform convergence","The test gives no information because $2$ is finite"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"eb361639247ff0d15c4ac49f","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_ratio_test_64a97320_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If the ratio limit does not exist, what does the ratio test conclude?","options":["The test is inconclusive","The series diverges","The series converges absolutely","The series has radius $+\\infty$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5a3e70f775414fb0b14ea612","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_ratio_test_64a97320_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $a_n = \\frac{2^n}{n}$, what is $\\displaystyle \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\ \\left|\\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\\right|$?","options":["$\\frac{1}{2}$","$2$","$1$","$+\\infty$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"75535f00bcd312804b644120","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_ratio_test_64a97320_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For the power series $\\sum_{n = 0}^{\\infty}2^n(x-3)^n$, what radius is obtained from $R = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}|c_n/c_{n+1}|$?","options":["$\\frac{7}{2}$","$3$","$2$","$\\frac{1}{2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cf6dd32d32aca6ce44008176","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_ratio_test_64a97320_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does $L = 1$ not decide convergence in the ratio test?","options":["Because at $L = 1$ every series converges, but only conditionally","Because $L = 1$ occurs only when the terms $a_n$ are eventually constant","Because both convergent and divergent series can have $L = 1$","Because $L = 1$ means the terms $a_n$ do not tend to $0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bda461e8ee51f8fc04911913","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_ratio_test_64a97320_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a power series, when may the ratio-test formula $R=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left|c_n/c_{n+1}\\right|$ be used?","options":["Only when that limit is a finite, nonzero number.","Only when the series is centred at $a=0$.","Whenever the coefficients $c_n$ tend to $0$.","When that limit exists or tends to infinity."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b60d08ba26ae20eb4d38dcec","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_ratio_test_64a97320_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For $\\sum_{n = 0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{n!}$, what radius does the ratio-test coefficient formula give?","options":["$+\\infty$","$0$","$1$","$\\mathrm{e}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bdf707ccf1acb672bfb71fdc","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_ratio_test_64a97320_005_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does the ratio test say about $\\sum a_n$ when $L=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}|a_{n+1}/a_n|=1$?","options":["The test is inconclusive.","The series must converge.","The series must diverge.","The series converges only if all $a_n$ are positive."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b4a17cac50c55b5fc27d620f","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_ratio_test_64a97320_016_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For the harmonic series $\\sum_{n\\ge1} 1/n$ the ratio of consecutive terms is $\\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}=\\dfrac{n}{n+1}$, which is strictly below $1$ for every $n$. What does the ratio test conclude?","options":["Nothing: the ratios tend to $L=1$, so the test is inconclusive.","The series converges, because every ratio is strictly below $1$.","The series diverges, because the ratios increase towards $1$.","The series converges absolutely, because the ratios are bounded above by $1$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e94140d1f7005e2fc859b168","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_ratio_test_64a97320_019_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For the power series $\\sum c_n(x-a)^n$ suppose $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}|c_{n+1}/c_n|=\\ell$ with $0<\\ell<\\infty$. Applying the ratio test to the terms $c_n(x-a)^n$, for which $x$ does it give $L<1$?","options":["$|x-a|<1/\\ell$","$|x-a|<\\ell$","$|x|<1/\\ell$","$|x-a|<1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5b1a9f23286106de37fcd375","time_limit":30}]}}